1 § 604. POWERS OF BOARDS OF COOPERATIVE EDUCATION
2 SERVICES EDUCATIONALSERVICE AREAS
3 (a) In addition to any other powers granted by law, a BOCES CESA shall
4 have the power to provide educational programs, services, facilities, and
5 professional and other staff that, in its discretion, best serve the needs of its
6 members, including professional development, curriculum coordination and
7 development, and transportation. A BOCES CESA shall follow all applicable
8 State and federal laws in its provision of services, including Section 504 of the
9 Rehabilitation Act of 1973, 29 U.S.C. § 794, and the Individuals with
10 Disabilities Education Act, 20 U.S.C. §§ 1400–1482. At a minimum, a CESA
11 shall offer services in the following areas to its members:
12 (1) special education, including implementation and maintenance of
13 tiered systems of support and the provision of low-incidence, high-cost
14 services;
15 (2) business and administrative services; and
16 (3) union school district creation consultation and facilitation.
17 (b) A BOCES may CESA shall employ an executive director who shall
18 serve under the general direction of the board and who shall be responsible for
19 the care and supervision of the BOCES CESA. The board shall annually
20 evaluate the executive director’s performance and effectiveness in
21 implementing the programs, policies, and goals of the BOCES CESA. The

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 15 of 318
1 executive director shall not serve as a board member, officer, or employee of
2 any related for-profit or nonprofit organization.
3 (c) A BOCES CESA shall be a body politic and corporate and shall have
4 standing to sue and be sued to the same extent as a school district. A BOCES
5 CESA may enter into contracts for the purchase of supplies, materials, and
6 services and for the purchase or leasing of land, buildings, and equipment as
7 considered necessary by the board of directors. Section 559 of this title shall
8 apply to the procurement of services or items with costs that exceed
9 $40,000.00, as well as high-cost construction contracts, as defined by
10 subsection 559(b) of this title.
11 (d) The board of directors of a BOCES CESA may apply for State, federal,
12 or private grants, for which a BOCES CESA may be otherwise eligible, to
13 obtain funds necessary to carry out the purpose for which the BOCES CESA is
14 established. Nothing in this chapter is intended to create an entitlement to
15 federal funds distributed by the Agency of Education to local education
16 agencies.
17 § 605. FINANCING, BUDGETING, AND ACCOUNTING
18 (a) Education cooperative fund. A BOCES CESA shall establish and
19 manage a fund to be known as an education cooperative fund. All monies
20 contributed by the member school districts and all grants or gifts from the

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 16 of 318
1 federal government, State government, charitable foundations, private
2 corporations, or any other source shall be deposited into the fund.
3 (b) Treasurer.
4 (1) A BOCES CESA shall appoint a treasurer who may be a treasurer of
5 a member school district and who shall be sworn in before entering the duties
6 of the office.
7 (2) The treasurer may, subject to the direction of the board of directors,
8 receive and disburse all money belonging to the board without further
9 appropriation.
10 (3) The treasurer shall keep financial records of cash receipts and
11 disbursements and shall make those records available to the board of directors
12 upon request.
13 (4) The board of directors shall ensure that its blanket bond covers a
14 newly appointed treasurer before the treasurer enters upon the duties of the
15 office. In lieu of a blanket bond, a BOCES CESA may choose to provide
16 suitable crime insurance coverage. The board of directors may pay reasonable
17 compensation to the treasurer for services rendered and shall evaluate the
18 treasurer’s performance annually.
19 (c) Financial accounting system. A BOCES CESA shall use the uniform
20 chart of accounts and financial reporting requirements used by supervisory
21 unions as its financial accounting system.

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 17 of 318
1 (d) Audit. Annually, a BOCES CESA shall cause an independent audit to
2 be made of its financial statements consistent with generally accepted
3 governmental auditing standards and shall discuss and vote to accept the audit
4 report at an open meeting of the board. The board shall transmit a copy of
5 each audit to the boards of its member supervisory unions.
6 (e) Annual statement. Annually, a BOCES CESA shall prepare financial
7 statements, including:
8 (1) a statement of net assets; and
9 (2) a statement of revenues, expenditures, and changes in net assets.
10 (f) Budget. A The board of cooperative education services a CESA shall
11 adopt a budget prior to the beginning of the fiscal year for which the budget is
12 adopted.
13 (g) Loans. A BOCES CESA may, upon approval of its members, negotiate
14 or contract with any person, corporation, association, or company for a loan
15 not to exceed the difference between the anticipated revenues for the current
16 fiscal year for the budget of the BOCES CESA and the amount credited to date
17 to said budget in order to pay current obligations. Such loan shall be
18 liquidated within sixmonths thereafter from monies subsequently credited to
19 said budget. The total principal, interest, and fees to be paid on such loan shall
20 not exceed the total amount of the authorized budget for the same length of
21 time.

