Sec. 18a. INTERACTIVE EDUCATION FUNDING CALCULATOR;
DEPARTMENT OF TAXES
On or before October 1, 2027, the Department of Taxes shall publish on its
website an interactive education funding calculator (interactive calculator)
that enables a school district or a union school district study committee to
calculate an estimate of the total education funding from the State it received
under Vermont’s current education finance system in fiscal year 2027 and the
estimated total education funding it would have received from the State under

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 51 of 318
the new financing formula had it been in effect in fiscal year 2027. The
interactive calculator shall reflect all education finance policy choices
represented in the total appropriated State funds each school district receives
under Vermont’s existing education funding formula enacted through July 1,
2027, that would have an impact on funding levels. In developing the
interactive calculator, the Department of Taxes shall consult with the Joint
Fiscal Office, the Agency of Education, and the Department of Finance and
Management. On or before August 1, 2027, the Department of Taxes shall
submit a preliminary plan for the interactive calculator to the Joint Fiscal
Office and the Agency of Education for feedback. The Department of Taxes
shall submit a preliminary version of the interactive calculator to the Joint
Fiscal Committee in September 2027.
1 * * * Prekindergarten Education * * *
2 Sec. 19. PREKINDERGARTEN EDUCATION; FINDINGS
3 The General Assembly finds that:
4 (1) despite being colloquially known as the “universal prekindergarten
5 program,” not all children three and four years of age in the State have equal
6 access to a prequalified prekindergarten provider;
7 (2) Vermont ranks second in the country with regard to access to
8 prekindergarten education by children who are four years of age, with
9 76 percent of eligible four-year-old children receiving prekindergarten

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 52 of 318
1 education, and that Vermont is one of two states in which more than 70 percent
2 of children who are four years of age receive prekindergarten services;
3 (3) only 11 percent of eligible children are enrolled in prekindergarten
4 services in EssexCounty;
5 (4) there is considerable geographic disparity in the State with regard to
6 the number of prekindergarten slots available, and as a result, 95 percent of
7 eligible children in Windsor and Windham Counties and 93 percent of eligible
8 children in Chittenden County have access to a prequalified prekindergarten
9 provider as compared to 55 percent in Franklin County and 61 percent in
10 Grand Isle County; and
11 (5) while a substantial portion of states provide a full school day of four
12 or more hours of prekindergarten education daily, less than five percent of
13 Vermont’s prequalified prekindergarten providers provide a full day of four or
14 more hours of prekindergarten education.
15 Sec. 20. LEGISLATIVE INTENT
16 It is the intent of the General Assembly to:
17 (1) ensure that prekindergarten education is included as an integral part
18 of Vermont’s education system, as the right to education is fundamental for the
19 success of Vermont’s children in all grades, prekindergarten through grade 12;
20 (2) determine a locus of responsibility to ensure there is access to
21 prekindergarten education within all school districts;

BILLAS PASSED BY THE HOUSE AND SENATE H.955
2026 Page 53 of 318
1 (3) provide access to licensed teachers in the classroom of both
2 prequalified public and private providers, including access to support and
3 provisional status; and
4 (4) equalize financial resources for all prequalified providers of
5 prekindergarten education.
